TY  - JOUR
T1  - Profiles of alcohol intoxication and their associated risks in young adults’ natural settings: A multilevel latent profile analysis applied to daily transdermal alcohol concentration data
AN  - 3086951982; 2025-09651-001
AB  - Objective: Transdermal alcohol concentration (TAC) sensors capture aspects of drinking events that self-reports cannot. The multidimensional nature of TAC data allows novel classification of drinking days and identification of associated behavioral and contextual risks. We used multilevel latent profile analysis (MLPA) to create day-level profiles of TAC features and test their associations with (a) daily behaviors and contexts and (b) risk for alcohol use disorders at baseline. Method: Two hundred twenty-two regularly heavy-drinking young adults (
M 
age = 22.3) completed the Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test (AUDIT) at baseline and then responded to mobile phone surveys and wore TAC sensors for six consecutive days. MLPA identified day-level profiles using four TAC features (peak, rise rate, fall rate, and duration). TAC profiles were tested as correlates of daily drinking behaviors, contexts, and baseline AUDIT. Results: Four profiles emerged: (a) high-fast (8.5% of days), (b) moderate-fast (12.8%), (c) low-slow (20.4%), and (d) little-to-no drinking days (58.2%). Profiles differed in the odds of risky drinking behaviors and contexts. The highest risk occurred on high-fast days, followed by moderate-fast, low-slow, and little-to-no drinking days. Higher baseline AUDIT predicted higher odds of high-fast and moderate-fast days. Conclusions: Days with high and fast intoxication are reflective of high-risk drinking behaviors and were most frequent among those at risk for alcohol use disorders. TAC research using MLPA may offer novel and important insights to intervention efforts. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
 (Source: journal abstract) Impact statementWe used a wearable sensor of intoxication and a novel analytic approach to characterize daily alcohol intoxication patterns among 222 regularly heavily drinking young adults (average age = 22.3). Our results showed that high-speed, high-peak intoxication characterizes young adults at higher risk for alcohol use disorder and is associated with heavy and risky drinking behavior. Our approach demonstrates how sensors of intoxication and consideration of multiple risk dimensions in analyses may advance the science and assist in the development of prevention and treatment efforts. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
