TY  - THES
T1  - Ambulatory assessment to study nondrug reward in recovery from opioid use disorder
AN  - 3168020878; 2025-54908-142
AB  - Opioid use disorder and overdose are among the most pressing public health problems in the United States over the last 20 years. People impacted by opioid use disorder know firsthand its complex and deeply painful challenges. Experiencing greater enjoyment and reward from experiences other than drug use (i.e., nondrug rewards) can promote the transition from addiction to recovery. Investigations of nondrug reward have occurred primarily within laboratories or clinics, however, which may limit generalizations about how nondrug reward experiences unfold in daily life. Ambulatory assessment, an approach for collecting a variety of subjective and objective data in daily life (e.g., smart-phone delivered surveys and GPS data collection) has the potential for assessing experiences within the time frames and contexts that nondrug rewards occur. In turn, this may guide digital interventions that promote greater seeking and savoring of nondrug rewards on a daily basis. My dissertation focuses on the use of ambulatory assessment to study nondrug rewards in recovery from opioid use disorder. This includes a conceptual/theoretical examination of using ambulatory assessment to study nondrug reward (Chapter 2) and two empirical studies based on a daily diary data collection where people in recovery from opioid use disorder answered daily surveys via text message for three weeks (Chapters 3 and 4). The aim of Chapter 3 was to examine daily factors (i.e., accessibility and motivation) associated with greater likelihood of experiencing nondrug reward. The aim of Chapter 4 was to characterize different types of daily experiences of nondrug rewards based on the degree to which they were pleasurable/rewarding, meaningful, immersive, and anticipated; then, to test if these experiences differentially predicted daily recovery outcomes. Participants (n = 153, 2,067 person observations; Mage = 41.5, SD = 9.9; 56% women, and 85% White and 15% Black or African American) were receiving opioid agonist treatments (methadone or buprenorphine) at a community addiction treatment center in Columbus, OH and most participants had a year or longer in recovery (63%). In Chapter 2, key benefits for studying nondrug reward with ambulatory assessment are discussed, including defining idiosyncratic experiences, examining within-person experiences, and insights into the real-world contexts of nondrug reward. In Chapter 3, greater between- and within-person (daily) motivation and access were associated with greater likelihood of experiencing nondrug reward. In Chapter 3, nondrug reward experiences varied distinctly across days, with nondrug rewards that were collectively the most meaningful, pleasurable, and anticipated being the strongest predictors of same-day positive recovery outcomes (i.e., improved mood and greater quality of life). Engagement with nondrug rewards varies based on changes in daily experiences, including accessibility—an often overlooked aspect of nondrug reward in addiction recovery. Nondrug rewards are highly heterogenous experiences that vary from day-to-day and differentially link to positive recovery outcomes. This dissertation provides preliminary insights into potential targets for promoting greater daily enjoyment and reward in recovery. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
