TY  - JOUR
T1  - French general practitioners and medical cannabis: A need for training
AN  - 2923422834; 2024-16329-018
AB  - Comments on an article by G.Akiki et al.(see record 2023-67318-007). Akiki et al.’s work on hospital physicians’ attitudes and knowledge regarding medical cannabis in Lebanon. Over 85 physicians, they found that most of them considered they would benefit from education on medical cannabis, and that such an education should be incorporated in their studies. Less than a quarter of respondents reported adequate knowledge of cannabis side effects, and 16% reported knowing the indications. In a country with – as Lebanon – non-legally-facilitated access to prescribed medical cannabis, we found that GPs’ knowledge on medical cannabis was not associated with sex, age or city size of practice. We therefore did not confirm Akiki et al.’s findings that physicians under 30 years had more knowledge than those over 30 years. However, our results suggest that knowledge is more associated with personal experiences and individual motivations. The fact that short training was not considered as an impediment to willingness to prescribe corroborates the results from Lebanon that suggested that physicians are eager to be trained on medical cannabis. Such a training could also be an opportunity to dissipate misconception and enable patients to have access to prescribed cannabis-based products. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
