TY  - JOUR
T1  - The importance of overlap: A retrospective analysis of electrical field maps, alcohol cue-reactivity patterns, and treatment outcomes for alcohol use disorder
AN  - 2841109349; 2023-85651-011
AB  - This letter presents a study which aims to conduct a retrospective analysis of electrical field maps, alcohol cue-reactivity patterns, and treatment outcomes for alcohol use disorder. In this retrospective analysis, the authors hypothesized that individuals wherein the TMS-induced electrical field overlapped with participant specific patterns of brain reactivity to alcohol cues would experience the best clinical outcomes. This retrospective analysis in individuals with AUD adds to the growing body of brain stimulation literature highlighting the importance of having TMS-induced electric field intersect with the neural networks of interest in a given patient population. In this study individuals who had an overlap between the TMS-induced electric field and endogenous alcohol cue-reactivity maps were more likely to maintain enrollment and remain sober 3 months after treatment than their counterparts wherein the TMS-induced electric field did not intersect their endogenous response to alcohol cues. Put in context with the larger field these data suggest that, when using a figure-of-eight TMS coil, targeting the location based on functional brain imaging may improve patient outcomes. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
