TY  - THES
T1  - Occupational stress in general practitioners
AN  - 2737159792; 2022-90099-264
AB  - This research investigates the sources of occupational stress and stress outcomes among General Practitioners (GPs) in England. Qualitative data were obtained from in-depth interviews with a random sample of 42 GPs. The quantitative data were then obtained by designing a survey, postal questionnaire based on the analysis of the interviews and other measures. The major sample consisted of 1817 GPs selected at random by 20 Family Practitioner Committees in England who returned completed questionnaires. The result showed that GPs have high levels of 'intrinsic' job satisfaction as a result of the autonomy and freedom of their working methods, women GPs both had job satisfaction and showed positive signs of mental wellbeing in contrast with other normative groups. Conversely, male GPs showed significantly higher anxiety scores than the norms, had less job satisfaction, and drank more alcohol than their female counterparts. Multivariate analysis disclosed four job stressors that were predictive of high levels of job dissatisfaction and lack of mental wellbeing; these were demands of the job and patients' expectations, interruption at home and work, job interfering with family life and practice administration. It was also found that the GP most 'at risk' in relation to mental ill health symptoms is someone who often adopts negative coping strategies. Finally, the research implications of the findings are presented with special emphasis on training and suggestion for providing a counselling service for GPs. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
