TY  - THES
T1  - Determine the relationship between nonmedical opioid use and mental functioning
AN  - 2582825886; 2021-80518-249
AB  - Background: Non-medical opioid use (NMOU) represents a public health issue that is increasingly important. Mental functioning is a significant concern hypothesized to be associated with NMOU and contributing to the opioid epidemic. Objectives: Determine if NMOU is associated with mental functioning by examining 1) spatial correlation between opioid use disorder (OUD) and mental disorders, 2) association of NMOU and cognitive function across lifespan, and 3) temporal ordering between NMOU and major depressive disorder (MDD). Methods: Missouri ZIP Health Rankings Project 2016 was derived for objective 1. Global and local spatial models were performed to examine the correlation between those health outcomes. National Survey on Alcohol and Related Conditions (NESARC) wave 3 was utilized for objective 2. Cognitive function was divided into two domains: attention and executive function. Time-Varying Effect Model was performed to examine the age-varying association between NMOU and cognition, and NESARC wave 1 and 2 were used. Baseline MDD and depressive symptoms were used to predict incident NMOU at follow-up. Baseline NMOU was used to predict incident MDD and symptoms at follow-up. Results: For objective 1, global spatial models showed significant positive relationships across the ZIP codes in Missouri. Local spatial model performed better than global spatial models according to AIC. For objective 2, NMOU was associated with decreased attention in middle age (37–54 years old) males (beta = –0.7, 95% CI = –1.1, –0.3), young and old females (24–28 years, beta = –0.4, 95% CI = –0.8, –0.05; 59–68 years, beta = –0.5, 95% CI = –0.9, –0.007 respectively). A significant decline in executive function was detected for males (18–50 years, beta = –0.7, 95% CI = –1.05, –0.3; and 68–83 years, beta = –1.2, 95% CI = –2.4, –0.08) and females (27 to 42, beta = –0.5, 95% CI = –0.8, –0.2; and 46 to 67 years, beta = –0.8, 95% CI = –1.2, –0.4). Baseline MDD diagnosis (aOR = 2.05, 95% CI = 1.71, 2.47) and symptoms (aOR = 1.25, 95% CI = 1.14, 1.38) were associated with higher odds of incident NMOU. Baseline NMOU was associated with higher odds of new-onset depressive symptoms (aOR = 1.42, 95%CI = 1.23, 1.63), but not MDD diagnosis at follow-up. Implications: This study would inform the development and implementation of prevention, treatment, and recovery efforts for tackling opioid use problems and mental disorders. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
