TY  - JOUR
T1  - Household surveys and the case of the Australian methamphetamine situation
AN  - 2587166939; 2021-83060-002
AB  - Comments on an article by Peter Reuter et al. (see record 2021-39157-001). Reuter et al. explored the important case of using general population surveys (GPS) to estimate frequent heroin use. The article discussed shortcomings inherent in household survey data including, under-reporting coinciding with increasing stigmatization of methamphetamine use, and a significant decline in the life-time prevalence of methamphetamine use. The current author argues that population surveys have their place however, we must become more sophisticated in understanding and correcting for their limitations, particularly considering the potential impact of changing survey technology and the uptake of on-line survey methods. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
