TY  - JOUR
T1  - Significance of integration and use of multiple data sources for understanding substance use and mental health disorders
AN  - 2587165758; 2021-83060-003
AB  - Comments on an article by Peter Reuter et al. (see record 2021-39157-001). Reuter et al. evaluate the prevalence of frequent heroin use from the National Survey on Drug Use and Health (NSDUH), a general population survey (GPS) at the Center for Behavioral Health Statistics and Quality (CBHSQ). CBHSQ at the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA) is the lead Federal government agency for behavioral health data and dissemination in the United States. The study emphasizes that underreporting and selective non-response in NSDUH can lead to underestimation. Claiming that these rare and stigmatized behaviors are prone to imprecise estimates and erratic year-to-year fluctuations because of small sample sizes, the authors recommend considering use of auxiliary data sources to help improve estimates. Here, Radhakrishnan argues that it is widely recognized that GPSs face these challenges, especially when measuring problematic drug use such as heroin. Nonetheless, the authors’ discussion raises the potential for misinterpreting the quality and representativeness of NSDUH data and more generally, the nature and use of the numerous GPSs conducted worldwide and relied on for public health policy decisions. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
