TY  - THES
T1  - Place and poverty: Using area deprivation index to assess the impact of socioeconomic disadvantage on health behaviors and outcomes
AN  - 2569671955; 2021-65614-004
AB  - BackgroundA growing body of literature suggests that environmental factors and social determinants have the potential to shape health outcomes. Despite the individual and policy implications of area-level deprivation for a wide range of clinical and public health outcomes, composite area-based measures have not been widely used to inform healthcare policy or clinical practice due to previously inaccessible and uninterpretable national geospatial data. However, traditional sociodemographic variables such as income, race/ethnicity, and sex do not fully capture the social determinants of health that play an instrumental role in shaping population health. Factors that operate beyond the individual, as a result of structural violence and geographic disparities, are often overlooked when making decisions at the national level (i.e. resource allocation) and even at the individual level (i.e. patient care). The area deprivation index (ADI) is a composite measure of social determinants of health comprised of 17 U.S. census indicators spanning four domains (income, housing, employment, education) that can be used to measure socioeconomic disadvantage for census-based regions. While we know that area-level social determinants play a role in population health, limited work has explored the impact of the neighborhoods where people live, completion of cancer screenings, opioid prescription fills and drug mortality, and events of severe dysglycemia.ObjectiveUse ADI to examine variability in health outcomes and health behaviors by area deprivation status across diverse health issues across the prevention continuum using diverse data sources and populations. Specifically, use ADI to explore primary care prevention at the patient/block group level (Aim 1), secondary injury prevention and mortality at the national/county level (Aim 2), and tertiary prevention through chronic disease management at the individual/county level (Aim 3).MethodsAim 1: Retrospective cohort study of adults receiving care across 75 primary care practices in Minnesota, Iowa, and Wisconsin. Rates of recommended breast, cervical, and colorectal cancer screening completion were ascertained through electronic health records from 2016-2017. The ADI was calculated for all block groups in Minnesota, Iowa, and Wisconsin (11,230 block groups). Using multivariable logistic regression, we examined the association between block group level ADI and completion of preventive cancer screening adjusting for ADI quintile, rural status, Charlson Comorbidity Index, age, sex (colorectal cancer only), and race/ethnicity.Aim 2: Retrospective cross-sectional study using the IQVIA Xponent data to capture opioid prescriptions and CDC National Vital Statistics System to assess drug-poisoning mortality. For all U.S. counties with available opioid prescription (2,712 counties) and drug-poisoning mortality (3,133 counties) data between 2012 and 2017, we used negative binomial regression to examine the association between county-level ADI and rates of opioid prescriptions and drug-poisoning mortality adjusted for year, age, race, and sexAim 3: Retrospective analysis of medical and pharmacy claims data from OptumLabs Data Warehouse (OLDW). Cohort acquisition was between January 1, 2016 and December 31, 2017 (look back period starting January 1, 2015 and follow up until December 31, 2018). We included all individuals who were ≥18 years of age, had an established diagnosis of diabetes mellitus, and had ≥12 months of insurance coverage. Diagnosis of diabetes was established using Healthcare Effectiveness Data and Information Set claims-computable criteria.ResultsAim 1: Patients living in the most deprived block groups were approximately half as likely to complete recommended cancer screening compared to patients living in the least deprived block groups. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2021 APA, all rights reserved)
