TY  - JOUR
T1  - Spaces and places in the lives of urban adolescents at risk for substance misuse
AN  - 1845548021; 2016-47673-001
AB  - This editorial discuses about the spaces and places in the lives of urban adolescents at risk for substance misuse.paces and places in the lives of urban adolescents at risk for substance misuse. Adolescents are willing to complete ecological momentary assessments (EMA) on smart phones, capturing their moods (anger, anxiety) and behaviors (smoking, marijuana), and the Global Positioning System (GPS) of the phone reveals to the researchers the longitude and latitude coordinates of their locations. Abrief motivational interviewing approach can be used to get adolescents to reflect on the risky places and peers with whom they are spending time and to consider reducing the amount of a substance they are using. A place-based approach, such as that of Michael Mason, can be useful to psychiatric-mental health nurse researchers and clinicians. Adolescent substance use/misuse, especially among urban adolescents who are exposed to risky places, is a significant mental health and societal problem. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
