TY  - JOUR
T1  - The promise of geospatial methods in prevention research
AN  - 1449311341; 2013-19430-002
AB  - Comments on an article by Ridenour et al (see record 2013-19430-008). The study by Ridenour et al represents an important step forward, both methodologically, and conceptually, for exploring associations among geospatial factors and various population characteristics. More specifically, these associations were used to inform the potential development and application of prevention services by demonstrating geographical areas where populations with the greatest needs can be found. The study raises the question of whether addiction attracts individuals to a particular neighborhood, or whether characteristics of certain neighborhoods lead to addiction among their inhabitants. The Ridenour study presents a logical plan for improving the situations for people in neighborhoods based on their cross-sectional data, in which prevention services can be most effectively delivered to people with targeted vulnerabilities in the neighborhoods where they cluster. Ridenour et al have demonstrated the importance of the geophysical world in sophisticated understanding of social problems in neighborhoods. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
