TY  - JOUR
T1  - Managing the continuum between pain and dependency in general practice
AN  - 909291528; 2011-11276-013
AB  - This article discusses that advocacy for improved management of pain has contributed to the escalation of prescription opioid analgesic (POA) use in the west over the last two decades. The most commonly prescribed class of medication is now the opioids. While they are essential for acute or terminal pain, there is limited evidence for their safety or efficacy in chronic non-malignant pain (CNMP), and population studies indicate that POAs do not seem to improve key outcomes such as: pain relief, quality of life or functional capacity. POA safety and efficacy studies have excluded those with past opioid use disorders, yet half of those with this diagnosis will move onto chronic opioid therapy (COT). Advantages of universal precautions (UPs) could include improved practice morale and security and decreased adverse drug-related behaviors (ADRBs). By tracking ADRBs, general practitioners may avoid the distress where a pain patient becomes recognised as an abuser. Potential disadvantages of UPs include the risk pain patients may feel stigmatised and then forego COT and the requirement to increase funding and attention by policy-makers. Thus while trying to improve the management of pain, GPs should aim to minimise the harms from POAs. Current intuitive approaches are not effective and a systematic approach of UPs is recommended. However, it is not realistic for GPs, or their employers, to implement such an approach, given the pressure on them to improve their time management. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
