TY  - JOUR
T1  - Unexplored territories
AN  - 742981462; 2010-09361-004
AB  - Comments on an article by P. Nilsen (see record 2010-09361-002). As found in the past, a major consideration when interpreting research findings is the difference between northern and southern Europe regarding alcohol research, and particularly research into BIs. As the references the author’s paper demonstrate, there is no published BI material generated in southern European countries, even if there are a few exceptions that focus upon primary health care or emergency rooms. This is due probably to a combination of both little tradition of alcohol studies and less concern about drinking in southern Europe, where the meaning of alcohol beverages, drinking patterns and presumably hazardous drinking is divergent from other European regions and English speaking countries. One obvious issue is the relation between prevention and clinical work as it is experienced by each GP. Doctors are trained essentially to treat individuals, while screening hazardous drinkers and intervening on them has more to do with life-styles, risk and probability, i.e. it compels the GPs to think collectively. Another obstacle to BI implementation is the perception and the awareness of alcohol problems by physicians. Experience and literature seem to suggest that when facing the issue of hazardous drinking, GPs can be inclined to become judgmental about the client and his/ her drinking behavior, raising guilt or shameful feelings in the consultation room. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
