TY  - THES
T1  - Impact of cognitive functioning backloading and neighborhood risk on HCV prevalence among injection drug users in Baltimore, Maryland
AN  - 837458833; 2010-99220-271
AB  - Objectives. Understanding how intellectual functioning, impulse control and neighborhood-level physical and social risk work together to impact infectious disease among injection drug users (IDU) will help to inform infectious disease prevention interventions, support the provision of educational tools that are appropriate for IDU groups with lower intellectual functioning and/or impaired impulse control, and reduce neighborhood disorder. The specific aims of this study were to examine: (1) the degree to which variations in intellectual functioning may modify the effect of backloading on HCV prevalence among IDU, (2) the relationship of impulse control with backloading, controlling for intellectual functioning, and (3) potential associations of neighborhood-level risk (physical and social disorder, evidence of alcohol and other drugs) with HCV prevalence. 
Methods. Aims 1 and 2 use the Baltimore sample of the International NEURO-HIV epidemiological investigation exclusively. Aim 3 merges the NEURO-HIV Baltimore data with neighborhood-level physical and social risk data collected using the Neighborhood Inventory for Environmental Typology (NIfETy) in Baltimore, Maryland. Of the total 676 injection and non-injection drug users in the Baltimore sample, analyses for Aims 1 and 2 were restricted to 436 injection drug users aged 16-50. Aim 3 was restricted to 219 injection drug users who reported existing intersections where they spend most of the time that could be mapped in Baltimore City. Logistic regression was used to estimate associations of cognition with HCV prevalence among injectors in both Aim 1 and Aim 2. Survey logistic regression for complex survey design, factor analysis and geospatial analyses are used in Aim 3 to examine associations among neighborhood-level risk indicators with HCV prevalence, controlling for individual level covariates, such as intellectual functioning and impaired impulse control. 
Main Findings. Backloading was associated with HCV seroprevalence (AOR = 6.51; 95% CI: 1.85-22.9) among those who tested in the lower three quartiles of intellectual functioning, but was not associated with HCV seroprevalence (AOR = 1.12; 95% CI: 0.38-3.26) among injectors who tested in the highest quartile of intellectual functioning. Injectors scoring in the top quartile of standardized commission errors of the Test of Variation in Attention (TOVA), indicating impaired impulse control, had twice the odds of backloading (AOR=2.11; 95% CI: 1.09 – 4.06) compared to those who scored in the lower three quartiles of TO VA commission errors. Since HCV prevalence was quite high (> 75%) and somewhat equally distributed among the participating injectors in Baltimore City regardless of where they spent their time, none of the quartiles of the neighborhood risk measure were statistically significantly associated with HCV in this sample. 
Conclusions. Knowledge of cognitive functioning and neighborhood-level physical and social risk factors that influence risk behavior and infectious disease among injection drug users may aid prevention efforts to include risk and harm reduction training at a level that is better understood and better received by injection drug users. HCV infection distribution among injectors is probably more influenced by social networks than physical environment. More research is warranted, however, to quantify disorder in urban neighborhoods and to evaluate structural interventions already in place to reconnect people to their neighborhoods and ultimately improve urban health outcomes. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
