TY  - JOUR
T1  - Thinking, feeling, attitudes and ethics: Core issues in psychology
AN  - 742996175; 2010-11132-001
AB  - Introduces the articles in the present issue of 
Australian Psychologist. Walker et al undertake a systematic review of the effects of education and cultural background on performance on WAISIII, WMS-III, WAIS-R and WMS-R measures. Dingwall and colleagues follow with an offering on the reliability of repeated cognitive testing in healthy Indigenous Australian adolescents. Vearncombe and Pachana round out the cognitive topics addressed in this issue in an article examining the extent to which physical health, treatment and psychological factors contribute to cognitive dysfunction after chemotherapy for breast cancer. The effects of alcohol versus placebo drinks in reducing feelings of sadness are explored in a novel study by Sitharthan and colleagues. Delineating a set of graduate attributes of the 4-year Australian undergraduate psychology program is the topic of a paper by Cranney and colleagues. Lewis and colleagues relate their systematic analysis of Australian professional and registration board standards, with the APS Code of Ethics (2007) emerging as the most comprehensive to date. The literature on positive psychology and adolescent well-being is explored by Norrish and colleagues, with a view to providing a cohesive platform for future research and discussion in the area. Finally, GPs’ views of the benefits of the new Medicare policy in increasing accessibility of psychologists, and their frustrations with the bureaucracy surrounding the use of this policy, is explored in a paper by Dempsey and Donaghue. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
