TY  - JOUR
T1  - In this issue
AN  - 621952668; 2009-19975-001
AB  - This article presents an overview of the articles presented in this issue. The Disaster Preparedness Task Force of the American Association for Geriatric Psychiatry (AAGP) was formed after Hurricane Katrina devastated New Orleans in August 2005. Disproportionately high rates of health decline, mental health effects, and suicides have been observed in elderly populations after disasters, while minimal research or policy attention has been paid. Second article discussed differences in knowledge about Alzheimer disease (AD) in different ethnic groups. Third article states that there is evidence that smoking is related to cognitive decline, but this relationship has not been examined among older Mexican Americans, one of the fastest growing populations in the United States. Fourth article examines predictors of depression on 3-year follow-up in a biracial community sample of 110 depressed persons aged 55 and over in Brooklyn, N.Y. Fifth article describes the process and challenges of developing a community/academic research partnership to reach underserved elders in need of depression treatment. Sixth article compares the expert diagnosis of incident dementia with the judgment of GPs. Seventh article examines the longitudinal relationship between self-reported cognitive difficulties, depression, and performance on the Dementia Rating Scale (DRS) in patients with cardiovascular disease. A range of psychiatric symptoms and cognitive deficits occur in Parkinson’s disease (PD), but co-occurrence of symptoms complicates our understanding of non-motor symptoms in this population. The last article investigated whether drops in blood pressure, which are common in older people and associated with such brain changes in animals, might be increased in depression in older people. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
