TY  - JOUR
T1  - Prevalence of excessive alcohol consumption in pregnancy
AN  - 742970238; 2010-05992-013
AB  - Alcohol consumption offers no benefits in relation to the outcomes of pregnancy. It is fetotoxic, teratogenic and has many toxic effects. It is important for general practitioners (GPs), obstetricians and midwives to identify women who suffer from problem drinking during pregnancy, so that proper support and counseling can be offered. The aim of this study was to assess the prevalence of women drinking alcohol during their pregnancy, and how many were drinking above the national recommended safe levels. The study also aimed to determine how many pregnant women were asked about their alcohol intake, and provided with advice and/or information about alcohol intake, by their GP, midwife or hospital doctor. A questionnaire was administered to women attending their 20-week fetal anomaly scan, based on the Alcohol Use Disorders Identification Test (AUDIT). Eighty-three women said that they were still drinking alcohol during this pregnancy; of these, 23 women were drinking more than the recommended level. The main limitation of this study is that the quantification of alcohol consumption was self-reported and subjective. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
