TY  - JOUR
T1  - Patients' and physicians' needs; Defining therapeutic gaps in severe chronic pain
AN  - 1171910036; 2012-21827-002
AB  - A number of common problems occur with chronic pain management. Firstly, and particularly with opioid analgesia, there may be difficulty in achieving sustained efficacy owing to the development of tolerance and hyperalgesia. Secondly, the management of neuropathic pain and the neuropathic component of mixed pain syndromes may not provide adequate analgesia. Both these issues are associated with increased side effects, because of the need to increase the analgesic dosage. Thirdly, opioid side effects often lead to low levels of compliance, inadequate dosing and discontinuation of treatment. It is worth noting that patients’ perceptions of their unmet needs correlate closely with those of general practitioners (GPs). Concerns still exist among both physicians and patients about opioid use. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
