TY  - JOUR
T1  - Smoking cessation in 10 seconds--A general practitioner's view: Comment
AN  - 622084182; 2008-00720-020
AB  - If we are managing to at least flag up smoking, what more can General Practitioners (GPs) do? Time pressures prevent us from offering a more protracted or intensive discussion to every smoker, which in any case appears to improve its effectiveness only marginally. Part of the power of GP intervention is surely that it is opportunistic, delivered on the spot. Even the 10-minute physician-delivered intervention described in this issue by Meyer 
et al. (see record 2008-00720-018) becomes unfeasible as a mass intervention measure, given time constraints in the real world. However, the other intervention in their study, computer-generated tailored letters, proved equally effective. Tailored letters--an answer to a maiden's prayer for busy GPs? Possibly, although before I pay for all those stamps I would like to be more sure about what is really working in this study. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
