TY  - JOUR
T1  - Prise en charge du problème alcool par le médecin généraliste: Impact de sa formation et de ses représentations
TT  - General practice management of alcohol-related problems. Impact of the general practitioner's training and representations
AN  - 621216516; 2006-05678-005
AB  - This study analyses the role played by the general practitioner's addiction medicine training on the frequency of management of alcohol abuse in general practice and the influence of the general practitioner's representations of alcohol-related problems. The 113 general practitioners of the 
Observatoire de la médecine générale (OMG) were asked to answer a questionnaire on their training and representations. We compared their replies with their computerized visit results (VR). The proportion of patients in whom an alcohol problem was indicated in the VR was globally very low (1.08% of the patients consulting these GPs in 2003), with marked differences between practitioners (ranging from 0.35% to 2.11%). 58% of GPs had received specific training in addiction medicine, but often only once and a long time ago for 38% of GPs. GPs trained in addiction medicine manage a significantly greater proportion of alcohol-related problems (1.29%) than those with no addiction medicine training (0.80%). Other difficulties in GP management of alcohol-related problems include the patient's and the doctor's reticence to discuss the subject, as well as a lack of time and the belief that such an approach would be useless. Some of these difficulties can be improved by training, while others are directly related to the doctor's representation of alcohol and alcohol-abusers. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
 (Source: journal abstract)
