TY  - JOUR
T1  - Internet Use: In Search of an Addiction
AN  - 621475128; 2006-20178-002
AB  - Innovative and impressive technological advances coupled with economies of scale have led to the mass availability of personal computers, mobile (cell) phones with integrated web-access, personal organizer and camera, and dedicated games and GPS devices. The potential for misuse and inappropriate and excessive use of certain computer applications has led to the expression of concerns related to the psychological and behavioral impact of the Internet on individuals. One of the article raises questions of fundamental importance regarding the validity of Internet addiction as a disorder. In particular, they point to the need to differentiate excessive use from dependency and to the absence of any conceptual or theoretical basis accounting for the development of Internet addiction. Researchers and clinicians can easily be seduced into believing that Internet users are addicted by the nature of the addicts' self-report accounts and terminology used to describe repetitive behaviors. Claims of excessive use due to a compulsive 'need' evaporate on closer critical questioning, turning rather into an ego-syntonic choice to engage in behaviors that provides stimulation and excitement in the absence of any resistance or attempt to cease. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2025 APA, all rights reserved)
