TY  - JOUR
T1  - Screening for alcohol misuse
AN  - 620331602; 2004-11935-023
AB  - Comments on an article by M. Aira et al. (see record 2003-00715-011). Their study identifies seven categories influencing the physician-patient dialogue for alcohol consumption. We recently have completed a study of Senior House Officer (SHO) attitudes to screening for alcohol misuse in Accident and Emergency (A&E) (127 SHOs over 5 years). Briefly, we compare the experiences of GPs and A&E staff under the headings identified. It is likely to be more problematic for general practitioners (GPs) to broach the subject of alcohol as they are community rather than hospital based. A&E practitioners are less likely to meet their patients socially. Prior knowledge concerning a patient's alcohol problem is an advantage that primary care physicians have over A&E staff. GPs are inhibited from asking about alcohol consumption by value judgements concerning appearance, age, sex and profession. A detailed analysis shows that primary care should embrace a similar system, rather than the GP attempting to deal with a common and potentially time/effort-exhausting problem on an ad hoc basis, especially as new alcohol strategies place increased emphasis on brief interventions, largely through primary care. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
