TY  - JOUR
T1  - An exploration of the problems faced by young women living in disadvantaged circumstances if they want to give up smoking: Can more be done at general practice level?
AN  - 620143610; 2003-06942-010
AB  - Explored the barriers faced by women living in deprived circumstances if they want to give up smoking and determined what help they would like at general practice level. The study was set in general practice in an area of socio-economic deprivation in North West Edinburgh. The practice has >10,000 patients. In the first stage of the study, GPs and nursing staff at the practice were asked about their opinions of the effectiveness of well-known smoking cessation interventions using a Likert scale format (n=23). In the second stage, a random sample of 18 female smokers in the 18-40 age group were contacted by post and asked to complete the following 4 measures: (1) the Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale; (2) a self-complete measure of knowledge of health risks for themselves and for babies and children of women smokers; (3) opinions on effectiveness of well-known smoking cessation interventions (as completed by GPs and nursing staff); and (4) an open-ended questionnaire, allowing qualitative responses about their smoking. The findings gave insights into how the women felt about their smoking habit, their knowledge of health risks, their mental health, the needs that smoking meets for them, the difficulties faced in attempts at smoking cessation and what they think would help. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
