TY  - JOUR
T1  - GPs facing reluctant and demanding patients: Analyzing ethical justifications
AN  - 619962285; 2003-00715-009
AB  - Assessed how general practitioners (GPs) would act with demanding patients and how they would justify their choice. Questionnaires with questions about preferred actions and justifying reasons were completed by 160 GPs in Slovenia and 200 GPs in Sweden using four vignettes: 1) a healthy patient reluctant to quit smoking; 2) a healthy patient demanding an X-ray; 3) a pulmonary cancer patient reluctant to quit smoking; and 4) a pulmonary cancer patient demanding immunotherapy. The majority of GPs would bring up the question about smoking with patients reluctant to quit. They justified their choice based on promotion of medical benefit and protection from harm. Swedish GPs were less inclined to bring up smoking than Slovenian GPs on the grounds of respect for self-determination and an enhanced relationship. With demanding patients, a minority of GPs would grant the healthy patient's request for an X-ray that was not medically motivated. Slovenian GPs were much more inclined to grant the request than were their Swedish colleagues. Enhancing the relationship and respect for self-determination were the most important reasons for granting the demands. When the demands were denied, the GPs mostly referred to promotion of fair distribution of resources. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
