TY  - JOUR
T1  - GP and patient predictors of PSA screening in Australian general practice
AN  - 619962044; 2003-00715-006
AB  - Examined general practitioner (GP) and patient variables associated first with men's prior uptake of prostate-specific antigen (PSA) screening and, subsequently, its initiation during an index consultation in general practice. Participants were 423 male patients aged 40-70 yrs. Men reported their previous PSA screening status. In addition, demographic and clinical data, including the presence of lower urinary tract symptoms (LUTS) was collected. Men also were mailed a questionnaire 2 days after their index consultation to ascertain whether the GP had discussed PSA screening for prostate cancer and other behaviors. GPs themselves completed questionnaires eliciting demographic and practice characteristics as well as their propensity to screen and understanding of the evidence about PSA testing. GP and patient study variables were modelled simultaneously in analyses. Eighty men (23.0%) reported previously having had a PSA screening test. Men were significantly and independently more likely ever to have had PSA screening if their regular GP reported a propensity to initiate screening. GP age also was independently associated with men's PSA screening status as was men's age and severity of LUTS. Current smokers were less likely ever to have had a PSA screening test. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
