TY  - JOUR
T1  - Discussion of NRT and other antismoking interventions in UK general practitioners' routine consultations
AN  - 620046913; 2003-03641-003
AB  - Investigated the quality of UK general practitioners' (GPs') advice against smoking, including how frequently they recommend nicotine replacement therapy (NRT) to smokers. Characteristics of smokers whom GPs recommended use NRT with smokers who did not receive GP recommendations were compared. The authors conducted a cross-sectional survey of 3,525 adult patients attending 35 UK GPs over a 16-mo period. 2,955 eligible patients completed preconsultation questionnaires, and 1,026 were regular smokers, with 927 of the regular smokers completing postconsultation questionnaires. Of those completing the questionnaires, 20.2% or 187 recalled discussion of smoking with their GPs and of these, 66.3% or 124 perceived that they had been given an unequivocal message to stop smoking. Although 60.4% of smokers smoked more than 10 cigarettes daily, only 17.6% of those recalling antismoking advice reported discussion of NRT. GPs discussed NRT with only a minority of smokers who might benefit from the therapy. Intending to give up smoking in the next 4 wks was the only variable independently associated with smokers' recall of discussing NRT. The study findings thus provide only limited information to support the notion that UK GPs recommend NRT in an evidence-based manner. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
