TY  - JOUR
T1  - Emergency psychiatric detentions in a Scottish health region--the use of sections 24 and 25 of the Mental Health (Scotland) Act 1984
AN  - 620044793; 2003-03798-005
AB  - This study examined significant differences in the type of patients detained in the community compared to those detained as inpatients. The study also assessed situational circumstances influencing the use of emergency detention orders within a mixed urban and rural population. Information was collected on 100 outpatients and 100 inpatients. Demographic data, previous psychiatric admissions and detentions, previous patient contact, consent to detention, alcohol and illicit drug use, the main psychopathology, diagnosis on discharge, police involvement, antisocial behaviors, pressures from the family members or nursing staff, details of psychiatric training, previous use of emergency detaining powers, whether additional psychiatric advice was sought, and the outcome of the detention were all recorded. Outpatient detentions by GPs were analyzed. Significant findings were previous psychiatric admissions and detentions. For outpatient recommendations by psychiatrists, the only finding of significance was the suspicion of illicit drug use at the the time of detention. For inpatient detentions, none of the recorded variables were found to significantly correlate with progression to extended detentions. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
