TY  - JOUR
T1  - Determinants of physicians' prevention-related practice: A comparison between Aarhus, Denmark, and Mainz, Germany
AN  - 620796311; 2005-05321-011
AB  - Aims: The study describes the self-reported interest of physicians in prevention-related practice with alcohol misusers in Aarhus in Denmark and Mainz in Germany, and identifies determinants of this interest. Methods: The study took the form of a postal anonymous survey, carried out between 25 January and 15 February 2000, of general practitioners (GPs) and hospital practitioners (HPs). The identified sample was n = S72, and the response rate 66% (n = 374). Results: A minority of physicians in Aarhus (35%) and a majority of physicians in Mainz (60%) were personally interested in preventive work. In both cities, a self-reported interest in preventive work was significantly correlated to having taken an advanced course on alcohol misuse within the last 12 months and having a personal belief in the ability of physicians to influence patients' attitudes to alcohol. Being a GP in Aarhus and being female in Mainz were significantly related to reporting interest in preventive work, too. Physicians' age and drinking 5+ drinks a day at least once monthly were not found to be dependent on interest in prevention. Conclusion: These findings provide an empirical baseline for a future comparative research on a physician's personal interest in prevention, which is of critical importance for the design of effective culturally relevant prevention interventions. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
 (Source: journal abstract)
