TY  - JOUR
T1  - Effect of a GP desktop resource on smoking cessation activities of general practitioners
AN  - 619876030; 2002-13426-013
AB  - Evaluated an intervention aimed at increasing the quantity and quality of brief opportunistic general practitioner (GP) advice to smokers encouraging and supporting quit attempts. A randomized controlled trial was conducted with two groups: control and GP desktop resource (GDR). Smoking cessation activities of GPs were assessed by an independent postal survey 1 month after distribution of the desktop resource. Ss were 107 GPs in West Dorset. GPs' self-reported rates of advising and counseling smokers on cessation over the previous week were assessed. The rate of opportunistic advice per week in the GDR group was 4.9, compared with 2.8 in the control group. The rate of giving counseling was also higher: 2.2 in the intervention group vs 1.0 in the control group. The proportion who had recommended or prescribed nicotine replacement therapy was greater, although not significantly (54% vs 46%). The findings indicate that the GDR can increase the rate of delivery of opportunistic advice and provision of counselling. Given the importance of this activity, a larger trial appears to be warranted to examine the long-term effect and the effect on cessation rates in patients. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
