TY  - JOUR
T1  - A new method for describing smokers' consulting behaviours which indicate their motivation to stop smoking: An exploration of validity and reliability
AN  - 619868444; 2002-12433-003
AB  - Examined the construct validity and inter-observer reliability of the Smokers' Motivation Code (SMC). Consultations between 29 general practitioners (GPs) and their patients were video-recorded, during which regular or occasional smokers discussed smoking with their GPs and their consulting behavior was coded using the SMC. The reliability of 3 different observers' codings was investigated. Construct validity was investigated by comparing smokers' consulting behaviors coded using the SMC with measures of motivation to stop smoking recorded on pre-consultation questionnaires. Two pairs of observers achieved good reliability when using the SMC to code smokers' consulting behaviors during a subset of 11 video-recorded consultations. Smokers displaying readiness behaviors were significantly more likely than others to report having tried to stop in the past year, thinking about or trying to stop and to agree that their health would improve if they stopped smoking. Smokers displaying resistant behaviors were significantly less likely to report thinking about stopping/trying to stop smoking. Overall results support the construct validity and inter-observer reliability of the SMC and identify some consulting behaviors which might indicate smokers' motivation to stop smoking. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
