TY  - JOUR
T1  - La douleur en médecine générale: Point de vue du médecin de la douleur
TT  - Pain in general medicine: Perspective from pain medicine
AN  - 620135668; 2003-06901-006
AB  - The aim of the study was to describe, to point out the pathological fields and determine the training needs, an epidemiological, prospective, multicentric and randomized study (20 centers of 10 GP investigators) was carried out in Greater Paris. On a daily basis practice (at doctor's office or on home calls), the data of any patient met were wrote down and gave details about pain patients. A relief-assessment questionnaire had to be sent back in pre-paid envelopes. Whatever the age or the sex, anyone having pain complaint was taken into account. Results showed; 1. 178 doctors (89%) gave exploitable observations. It's a higher rate than literature; 2. 43% of GPs think they weren't enough trained; 3. Pain was met in 1408 cases out of 3291 (43%); 4. pain occurs whatever the meeting place, sex or age except for children under 2 (19%) and over 65 (39% as opposed to 70% in literature); 5. most frequent pains were: 37% rheumatology, 28% ear-nose-throat, 14% digestive, 11% headache and migraine. Only 1% cancer pain. 18,4% had more than one pain; 6. Chronic pain was in 20%, and 50% in rheumalology; 7. Pain caused school or work to be stopped for 29%; 8. 7% were refered to a specialist, whereas only 0.3% to a pain-relief center; 9. Level I antalgics and NSAI are given for shorter periods than level II and III antalgics. The latter are rarely given (1%), only to cancer patients; 10. 1037 patients (73.7%) answered the efficiency assessment quiz., 95% followed the course of treatment; 11. 86% felt relieved within 4 days, 30% got a 100% relief, 11% got no relief specially in rheumatology,: 12. Pain scales quiz were only used in 6%. In General Practice, pain is frequently met in Rheumatology, earnose- throat, digestive. The great involvement in both GPs and patients,, chosen by random, strengthen the validity of methodology and results. GPs consider they are not enough pain-trained. 80% patients were pain-relieved. Strong opioids are very rarely given (cancer). Training doesn't seem to suit daily practice. Are pain scales inadapted to GP or only misused? (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
 (Source: journal abstract)
