TY  - JOUR
T1  - Evaluating the alcohol environment: Community geography and alcohol problems
AN  - 619916682; 2002-17645-005
AB  - Discusses the geography of community alcohol use and related problems, and how research techniques may be used to prevent alcohol-related problems. The statistical procedures of spatial analysis and geostatistics help to avoid misleading interpretation of geospatial data. Sound geomathematical models are essential for transforming statistical studies of the community geography of drinking and problems into significant social policy recommendations. Current important areas of relevant research include mapping the alcohol environment, relating these features to the spatial distribution of problem events, and analyzing statistical associations between these measures and drinking behaviors. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
