TY  - JOUR
T1  - Impact of alcohol education and training on general practitioners' diagnostic and management skills: Findings from a World Health Organization collaborative study
AN  - 619643331; 2001-09087-008
AB  - The potential of general practitioners (GPs) to reduce the prevalence of alcohol-related problems (ARPs) via alcohol intervention (AI) contrasts sharply with actual practice. One explanation for GPs' limited involvement in AI is that they have had inadequate training or continuing medical education (CME) on ARPs. This study examined GPs' experience of alcohol-related CME an its possible relationship with attitudes and practice behavior regarding ARPs. A questionnaire was returned by 2,139 GPs from 13 countries. Diagnostic and management skills were assessed by responses to standardized case vignettes. About one third of GPs (32%) reported receiving no alcohol-related CME, while 8% could no remember whether or not they had received any such training or education. 23% reported less than 4 hr and 37% reported 4 or more hours of alcohol-related CME. GPs who reported higher levels of alcohol-related CME were more likely to obtain information about alcohol, were more prepared to counsel problem drinkers and managed more patients for alcohol issues than did colleagues reporting lower levels of CME. Those with greater CME experience were also more confident about their ability to alleviate ARPs and reported more appropriate management strategies than did GPs with less CME experience. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
