TY  - JOUR
T1  - Implementing guidelines for smoking cessation advice in Australian general practice: Opinions, current practices, readiness to change and perceived barriers
AN  - 619667264; 2001-16588-001
AB  - Investigated opinions, current practices, likely readiness to change and perceived barriers to change among Australian general practitioners (GPs) in order to develop a plan to implement national guidelines for smoking cessation advice. A postal survey of randomly selected GPs (aged 24–72 yrs) in New South Wales, Australia was carried out. 311 questionnaires were returned. Results show that only 34% of respondents reported providing cessation advice during every routine consultation with a smoker, in accordance with national guidelines. Specific evidence-based approaches recommended in guidelines were under-utilized, with only 54%"always" or "frequently" arranging follow-up, 32% providing written materials and 28% setting a "quit date". Ss were no more likely to advise quitting completely than the less effective method of nicotine fading. More than 1 in 4 Ss indicated readiness to change their behavior. Ss rated their patients' lack of motivation and uninterest as the most important barriers to smoking cessation advice in general practice. Neither uncertainty about effective smoking cessation strategies nor lack of reimbursement for smoking cessation advice were identified as barriers. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
