TY  - JOUR
T1  - Health beliefs and pap smears among Thai women in Brisbane, Australia
AN  - 619899627; 2002-17198-003
AB  - Examined health beliefs related to the use of Pap smears among Thai women in Australia. 145 1st-generation Thai migrant females residing in Australia completed questionnaires concerning demographic characteristics, drinking, smoking, and cervical cancer screening. Results show that 39% of Ss had undergone biennial tests within the previous 5 yrs, 23% had undergone 1+ tests but had not undergone testing within the previous 5 yrs, and 4% intended to undergo testing within 1 mo of the study. Self efficacy was positively associated with Pap smear testing. Ss aged 40+ yrs were more likely to have undergone Pap smear testing than were younger Ss. Those Ss having female general practitioners (GPs) were less likely to have undergone Pap smear testing than were those having a male GP. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2023 APA, all rights reserved)
