TY  - JOUR
T1  - Smoking behavior and opinions of French general practitioners
AN  - 619677023; 2001-17243-001
AB  - Examined smoking prevalence, sociodemographic factors, and the opinions of French general practitioners (GPs) concerning tobacco control policies. 1013 GPs (aged <35–49+ yrs) completed telephone questionnaires concerning medical practice, sociodemographic characteristics, and opinions about health behavior. Results show that 34.1% of Ss were current smokers. A higher proportion of males smoked compared to females; males consumed on average more cigarettes per day. 52.4% of Ss regarded their role in reducing nicotine addiction to be important. Those who considered their role as limited were less likely to advise pregnant women to stop smoking. Nonsmoking Ss were more supportive of bans on smoking in public places. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
