TY  - JOUR
T1  - Australia's mental health: An overview of the general population survey
AN  - 619537067; 2000-15397-001
AB  - Estimated the 1-mo and 1-yr prevalence of mental disorders in the Australian adult population and determined the amount of disablement associated with this. The use of health and other services by persons with common mental disorders was also determined. For the Adult Survey, a household sample of 10,600 persons aged 18 yrs and over were interviewed. The interview consisted of the composite international diagnostic interview in its automated presentation and other components to determine disablement, use of services and satisfaction with services received. The diagnostic classifications used in the analyses were both International Classification of Diseases (ICD)-10 and Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders-IV (DSM-IV). Only the results from ICD-10 are reported here. Results show a total of 17.7% of the sample had 1 or more common mental disorders, anxiety, depression, alcohol or substance abuse and neurasthenia. This morbidity was associated with considerable disablement in daily life: 3 days of impaired social role performance in the previous 4 wks, compared with 1 day for the general population. Of all cases, 64.6% had had no contact with health services in the previous year; 29.4% had seen GPs and 7.5% had seen psychiatrists. It is concluded that morbidity is associated with disablement, but most of it is untreated. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
