TY  - JOUR
T1  - Impaired health status, and mental health, lower vitality and social functioning in women general practitioners in Sweden: A cross-sectional survey
AN  - 619614385; 2001-06772-003
AB  - Analyzed the differences between 1,004 female and male general practitioners (GPs) in health status and lifestyle and compared this data with 5,443 adults from the general population in health-related quality of life. Participants completed measures of self-reported health status, smoking habits, physical activity, and quality of life. Age groups of 30–44, 45–49, and 50–64 yrs were compared. Results show that female GPs had a higher risk than male GPs of impaired health status. Of the female GPs ages 45–49 yrs, 22.8% reported that their health was impaired vs 12.6% of male GPs. Female GPs had higher means for physical functioning but lower means for vitality, social functioning, and role emotional and mental health vs the general population. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
