TY  - JOUR
T1  - French general practitioners' attitudes and reported practices in relation to their participation and effectiveness in a minimal smoking cessation programme for patients
AN  - 619385690; 1999-00751-022
AB  - Examined the participation and effectiveness of GPs in offering a smoking cessation intervention. 2,860 GPs were surveyed about their practices and attitudes and then matched pairs of smoking and non-smoking doctors were invited to participate in a regional smoking cessation intervention. From 371 smoking GPs and 375 non-smoking GPs invited to take part in a smoking cessation trial with patients, 170 smokers and 202 non-smokers participated. The GPs' attitudes and reported professional and personal practices were assessed in a telephone interview. Responses were compared with the GPs' participation in the cessation trial, and with GP "success" (1 or more patients stopping smoking at 1 mo, 12 mo or both) or "non-success" (no patient cessation). A lower proportion of smokers than non-smokers among the GPs who initially accepted did not participate in the study but thereafter, there were no significant associations between GPs' reported smoking practices and attitudes and the extent of their participation or effectiveness. Results indicate that, when minimal advice has an effect, it is due more to the systematic nature of the provision of the intervention than to the attitudes or reported practices of the practitioner providing the advice. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
