TY  - JOUR
T1  - Predictors of smoking cessation following physicians' counseling
AN  - 619337313; 1998-02866-010
AB  - The purpose of this study was to identify predictors of quitting following general practitioners' (GPs') anti-smoking counseling. The authors studied determinants (characterized following the Precede framework) of successful quitting (1 yr sustained abstinence) among 861 smokers randomized to intervention groups based on repeated counseling (RC), RC plus spirometric testing, and RC plus nicotine gum. Three sets of determinants of successful quitting were assessed: (1) predisposing factors, including attitudes and beliefs, and sociodemographic variables, (2) enabling factors, and (3) reinforcing factors. GPs' intervention worked best for male and married smokers, for smokers who had maintained abstinence for at least 1 mo in the past or at their first quit attempt, and for those who spontaneously reduced their coffee consumption; heavy smokers and those living with other smokers were less likely to quit. Previous antismoking advice by the GP represented a strong barrier to success for healthy smokers, but not for those reporting symptoms of shortness of breath. There were no interactions between predictors and treatment conditions. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
