TY  - JOUR
T1  - Smoking-cessation strategies observed in videotaped general practice consultations
AN  - 619279027; 1998-00055-001
AB  - Used a recent and representative videotape database to describe the frequency and content of opportunistic smoking-cessation counseling in Australian general practice. Experts rated the effectiveness and feasibility of smoking-cessation techniques by 70 general practitioners (GPs) among self-reported smokers. GPs identified smokers in 32% of consultations and counselled them in 29%. Median counselling time was 44 sec in encounters typically lasting 10 min. When smoking was raised, advice to quit, personalizing risks, discussing health risks, and quantifying consumption were the most commonly used strategies. Assessment of motivation to quit, individualized education, practical hints to stop, written materials, and followup were observed in a third or less of interventions. Quit dates and nicotine replacement were never proposed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
