TY  - JOUR
T1  - Why are some doctors high-prescribers of benzodiazepines and minor opiates? A qualitative study of GPs in Norway
AN  - 619217830; 1997-43723-003
AB  - Studies have shown great variations in prescribing volume and prescribing patterns of benzodiazepines and minor opiates among general practitioners. This study aimed to form a basis for hypotheses about prescribing, in order to investigate how high-prescribing doctors can legitimize their own prescribing pattern. A qualitative interview study of 38 doctors (aged 33–66 yrs) with previously known high, medium, and low prescribing volumes was performed. The interpretation focused on the doctors' self-explanations and how they influence their daily decision of prescribing. Results show that many of the attitudes towards the drugs were common both within and between the 3 groups of prescribers. All doctors regarded the task of prescribing as difficult, and the great majority strongly advocated restriction in prescribing. In order to cope with daily practice and with high prescribing volumes, doctors make use of effective working strategies. These strategies, of ascribing responsibility to the previous doctor, to patient autonomy and responsibility, to the patient's age, and to concomitant diseases, are described. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
