TY  - JOUR
T1  - Mental health in primary care: An epidemiological study of morbidity and use of health resources
AN  - 619102887; 1997-06082-010
AB  - Analyzed the sociological, clinical, and illness-related correlates of mental illness in primary care, and examined, during 1-yr follow-up, outcome and use of health resources. The general practitioner's (GP) ability to identify and handle their patient's mental illness also was examined. The survey comprised a 2-phase cross-sectional study. In the 1st phase 823 patients were classified using the General Health Questionnaire-28 or by the GP. In the 2nd phase 203 Ss from the 1st phase were assessed by the Schedules for Clinical Assessment in Neuropsychiatry (SCAN) system. Results show that the prevalence rate of mental illness (in attenders) using the GHQ was 33.2%. The corresponding rate for the GP was 14.1%, and 31.5% for the SCAN. Mental illness mainly comprised depression, anxiety, and alcohol-related diagnoses. The presence of mental illness and the use of health resources during follow-up were dependent on demographic characteristics and on their original psychiatric status. It was concluded that in primary care, mental illness constitutes a major health problem. Despite this fact, GPs do not recognize a substantial proportion of these health problems. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2018 APA, all rights reserved)
