TY  - JOUR
T1  - Attitudes of general practitioners to child psychiatry services
AN  - 619096084; 1997-05237-002
AB  - Examined the views of Irish general practitioners (GPs) with regard to local child psychiatry provision. 74 randomly selected GPs completed a questionnaire that asked to rate service provisions, professional staff, and therapies offered in terms of priority. Results show written reports, short waiting list times, and emergency inpatient provisions were accorded the highest priority by the largest number of GPs. Expertise in child sexual abuse, mental handicap and alcohol/drug addiction were also prioritized. The core staffing required included child psychiatrists, community psychiatry nurses, psychologists and social workers. Family therapy followed by counselling were the most popular treatment modalities. However, more than two-thirds of the GPs rated their current service as unsatisfactory. It is concluded that in planning service provision it is important to take into account the needs of the community, referrers' views and accessibility in order to provide optimum services for both users and referrers. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
