TY  - JOUR
T1  - Sampling for qualitative research using quantitative methods: 2. Characteristics of GPs who agree to video-taping of consultations
AN  - 619056289; 1997-02522-007
AB  - Describes the recruitment of male and female general practitioners (GPs) to a research project which involved video-recording their consultations to determine how GPs approach the topic of smoking cessation with patients. The project involved video-recording of GPs' surgeries and semistructured interviews with GPs using the Attitudes to Smoking Advice Questionnaire. 468 GPs were given the questionnaire; GPs refusing to participate were asked about their reasons. Results showed that 53 out of 123 Ss who were eligible to take part, agreed. GPs who agreed were younger and more likely to work in teaching or training practices and to be current members of the Royal College of General Practitioners. Lack of time and being unhappy with the use of video recording (VDR) were cited as the most common reasons for nonparticipation. Thus, some amount of self-selection seem to occur when GPs are asked to take part in research which involves VDR. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
