TY  - JOUR
T1  - Sampling for qualitative research using quantitative methods: 1. Measuring GPs' attitudes towards discussing smoking with patients
AN  - 619048503; 1997-02522-006
AB  - Describes the development of a questionnaire which measures general practitioners' (GPs) attitudes towards discussing smoking with patients, with the intention of using this instrument to select GPs with diverse views for a qualitative interview study. The (13 attitude Attitudes to Smoking Advice Questionnaire statements with an accompanying Likert-type scale) was completed by 327 GPs. Factor analysis of responses produced 2 subscales: 'perceived efficacy,' and 'enthusiasm.' The construct validity of the subscales was investigated by comparing attitude scores of GPs who reported giving different amounts of anti-smoking advice in their last surgery. Results showed that each subscale had good internal reliability and that the subscales were valid. The use of this type of instrument in sampling GPs for qualitative studies could be effective for selecting Ss with a diversity of views towards the research topic. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
