TY  - JOUR
T1  - A study of drug misusers' contacts with general practitioners
AN  - 619405710; 1999-05105-002
AB  - Surveyed 136 drug misusers (mean age 26 yrs) attending a community drug team and 15 primary health care workers, principally general practitioners (GPs) in the same district, to investigate the experience of general practice from a misuser's perspective and to see if certain misuser characteristics influenced GP's perceived response. 88% of the drug misusers were currently registered with a GP. In the last 5 yrs, 5 had registered with no GPs in that time, 93 with 1 or 2, 26 with 3 or 4, and 10 with 5 or more. Of those misusers who had a current GP, 107 said their GP knew they misused drugs, and of these, 75 said their GP's attitude had changed as a result of this new knowledge. Of the 119 misusers with a current GP, 34 believed their doctor was willing to prescribe methadone. Of those 85 who thought their GP was not currently willing to do so, 33 had prescribed methadone before. For tranquillisers, the results were 47 thought currently willing to prescribe, and 26 of those thought no longer willing to prescribe had previously done so. Data from the survey of 15 primary care providers suggest the response of GPs to opiate misusers may be related to their attitudes towards them as a group rather than as individuals. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
