TY  - JOUR
T1  - Correlates of physician counseling associated with obesity and smoking
AN  - 618850743; 1996-04873-002
AB  - Examined patient, physician, and consultation variables associated with overweight and smoking counseling in general practice consultations. Ss were 7,161 patients aged 18–75 yrs of 230 general practitioners (GPs) in Australia. Self-reported data from doctors and patients were collected via a questionnaire. It was found that identification of overweight was associated with, among others, being female and being heavier. Counseling for overweight was associated with being younger, being previously counseled, presenting with an associated condition, presenting for a routine check-up, visiting a GP who had longer consultations, having BP measured, etc. Identification of smokers was associated with, among others, being a heavier smoker and having been previously counseled. Counseling for smoking was associated with younger patients, longer consultations, previous counseling, BP measurement, presenting with an associated condition, and not presenting frequently. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
