TY  - JOUR
T1  - Standardized patients as a measure of change in the ability of family physicians to detect and manage alcohol abuse
AN  - 618819254; 1996-02554-001
AB  - Tested the impact of an educational program on the ability of family physicians (FPs) and general physicians (GPs) to detect and manage alcohol-related problems. 14 experimental FPs, 12 control FPs, 12 experimental GPs, and 16 control GPs were assessed for physician knowledge and skills before and after the educational intervention, on a scoring sheet containing 20 items (10 in the process domain and 10 in the content domain). Standardized patients (SPs) were used as the primary method of measuring changes in Ss' knowledge and skills. Pre- and post-test scores were calculated for each of the 20 items, based on the SPs' interviews before and after the intervention. The experimental GP group improved significantly more than the control, particularly in the content domain. However, pretest scores for the FP experimental group were marginally higher than those for the GP experimental group. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
