TY  - JOUR
T1  - The medical profile of unidentified problem drinkers in general practice: Test of an hypothesis
AN  - 619344571; 1998-04918-007
AB  - Tests the hypothesis that the medical profile specified by the standard also applies to unidentified problem drinkers. All known problem drinkers in the practices of 16 general practitioners (GPs), as well as a 1 in 10 random sample of patients considered to be non-drinkers were admitted to the study at their 1st surgery visit during a 1-yr period. Hidden problem drinkers were detected by means of a screening questionnaire, although the results were not conveyed to the GP until the study was completed. Over the 1-yr study period the GPs then registered all 35 Reasons for Encounter (RFEs) and Evaluations (Es) of the study population. RFE and E sum scores were then constructed based on the Alcohol Standard. The estimated population prevalence of problem drinking, corrected for the 1 in 10 sample fraction was 7%. The authors found 6% of problem drinkers in the category regarded by the GPs as non-problem drinkers. Differences in RFEs and Es between hidden problem drinkers and those regarded as non-problem drinkers were significant for irregular heartbeat and psychological problems. Sexual problems were significant at the RFE level, social problems at the E level. When identified problem drinkers are compared with non-problem drinkers more differences in the medical profile are found. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
