TY  - JOUR
T1  - Exploring the iceberg of morbidity: A comparison of different survey methods for assessing the occurrence of everyday illness
AN  - 618978628; 1996-92536-001
AB  - Examined problems in measuring the occurrence of acute symptoms of ill health. Data were from the Dutch Survey of General Practice, a nationwide study among 161 GPs. A random sample of 100 patients/GP was approached for a health interview and asked to keep a structured health diary for 3 wks. High levels of psychological distress increased the likelihood of recording symptoms for both instruments, but the increase was greater for the interviews. Ss who had only received limited education, heavy smokers, and those who suffered from chronic conditions had a significantly lower likelihood of recording symptoms in the diary as compared to the questionnaire. Psychological distress had a great effect on the response pattern for psychological symptoms, but not for musculoskeletal symptoms. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
