TY  - JOUR
T1  - Identifying suicidal ideation in general medical patients
AN  - 618661500; 1995-30598-001
AB  - Analyzed characteristics of general medical patients to help general practice physicians (GPs) decide which patients might need in-depth questioning about suicidal thoughts (STs). Survey questionnaires completed by 6,041 Ss in the National Institute of Mental Health Epidemiological Catchment Area study were examined. 154 (2.6%) Ss reported STs during the previous year. Ss with STs were more likely to have had psychiatric or medical hospitalizations in the past year. Also Ss with STs were more likely to have met Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders-III (DSM-III) criteria for panic disorder, major depressive disorder, drug and alcohol disorder, and phobia disorders in the past year. Results indicate that the best way to prevent suicide may be for GPs to improve their recognition and management of psychiatric conditions. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
