TY  - JOUR
T1  - General practice involvement in the management of alcohol misuse: Dynamics and resistances
AN  - 618603704; 1995-07267-001
AB  - Examines empirical evidence on the effectiveness of interventions offered to patients with potential or established alcohol problems by general practitioners (GPs) and describes barriers to the treatment of alcohol problems in the primary-care setting. These barriers are both practical and attitudinal in nature and stem from GPs, patients, the operational practices, and the ethos of the primary health care system itself. GPs may have difficulty in diagnosing alcohol problems and may feel that they should not be involved in the prevention and management of alcohol problems. Patients are often reluctant to seek help for alcohol problems and may question the appropriateness of GP treatment. Situational barriers include the already heavy workload of the GP and the changing nature of the doctor–patient relationship. Recommendations for overcoming these barriers are outlined. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
