TY  - JOUR
T1  - General practitioners as agents of health risk behaviour change: Opportunities for behavioural science in patient smoking cessation
AN  - 618643394; 1995-22739-001
AB  - Uses the model proposed by L. W. Green et al (1988) to describe the circumstances under which general practitioners (GPs) currently practice the role of modifying patient health risk behaviors, specifically smoking. Research has identified predisposing factors (practitioner knowledge and attitudes, and community attitudes), enabling factors (efficacy/effectiveness of smoking cessation interventions, availability of clinical guidelines, and practice organization), and reinforcing factors (financial reimbursement, performance feedback, and peer support) that impede the routine application of smoking cessation strategies by GPs. Opportunities for behavioral scientists to improve the circumstances under which GPs practice this form of care exist in the development and evaluation of interventions and the improvement of practitioner attitudes and knowledge. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2020 APA, all rights reserved)
