TY  - JOUR
T1  - Asian inpatients in a district psychiatric unit: An examination of presenting features and routes into care
AN  - 618447433; 1994-15009-001
AB  - Examined the case notes of 100 consecutive Asian admissions at first presentation to a district psychiatric unit. Asian admissions to the unit were comprised of a young, well-educated group of predominantly Punjabi Sikhs. They present mostly through crisis services, often following a domiciliary visit. General practitioners (GPs) were involved in only 34% of referrals, although 54% of patients had Asian GPs. These inpatients were less likely to be compulsorily admitted than has previously been reported, and they rarely came into contact with police services. Alcoholism was found mostly in male married inpatients. Psychotic illness accounted for 51% of presentations and neurotic depression for 18%, the latter being the largest single diagnostic category. Results raise questions regarding preferred routes into care for Asians. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
