TY  - JOUR
T1  - Prevalence of smoking in physicians and medical students, and the generation effect in the Netherlands
AN  - 618324691; 1993-35184-001
AB  - Of 789 Ss who responded to an anonymous, self-administered postal survey, 38% of the general practitioners (GPs), 27% of the consultants, 18% of the medical students, and 31% of the health policy and management (HPM) students were smokers. The prevalence of smoking was higher in the male GPs and in the male HPM students than in the general male population. The prevalence of smoking was lower in female physicians and students than in their male counterparts and also lower than in the general female population. Medical students were not inclined to start smoking. This strong generation effect will reinforce the current downward trend in smoking prevalence in Dutch physicians. The doctors had a suboptimal level of knowledge about methods of smoking cessation and about the association between smoking and health disorders. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
