TY  - JOUR
T1  - The effectiveness of lifestyle-related health screening: A 2 year follow-up study of doctors and teachers
AN  - 618302996; 1993-26949-001
AB  - Assessed the effects of screening 49 GPs and 49 teachers for fitness, body mass index, serum cholesterol, and lifestyle measures such as smoking, alcohol consumption, and diet and exercise habits after 6 and 24 mo. There was no significant difference for any screened measure between 0 mo and 6 or 24 mo except that Ss found to have a raised cholesterol at 0 mo were subsequently more likely to reduce the proportion of fat in their diet. There were no significant differences between screened Ss compared with a control group of 74 nonscreened doctors and 54 teachers at 24 mo. The number of Ss with abnormal measurements found at the screening session who altered to measurements within the normal range was equal to the number of Ss who changed from normal to abnormal measurements over the 2-yr period of study. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
