TY  - JOUR
T1  - Recruitment and participation of general practitioners in a multipractice study of smoking cessation
AN  - 619302762; 1998-01750-005
AB  - Examined some factors that distinguish general practitioners (GPs) who participate in a smoking cessation study from those who do not participate. 398 GPs in Norway were invited by letter to participate in a 3-yr multipractice intervention study of smoking in pregnant and nonpregnant women. After the intervention study, 178 participating and 173 nonparticipating GPs were sent a questionnaire on doctor and practice characteristics, smoking habits, and attitudes to health promotion and health risks in general practice. Results showed that working in a group, working on a regular salary, having started specialization in general practice, being less than 40 yrs of age, and being a nonsmoker were significantly more common among the participating GPs. They also had significantly fewer consultations per wk than nonparticipating GPs. Furthermore, smoking habits predicted participation in the study far more than any other predictor. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
