TY  - JOUR
T1  - Engaging patients with alcohol problems in treatment: The first consultation
AN  - 618165723; 1992-40672-001
AB  - Examined the impact of the 1st consultation at a specialist alcohol clinic on patients' perceptions of their drinking problem and on their expectations of help (EOH) from 3 sources: an alcohol clinic (AC), general practitioners (GPs), and Alcoholics Anonymous. 40 Ss completed the General Health Questionnaire; Severity of Alcohol Dependence Questionnaire (T. Stockwell et al, 1979); and Alcohol Problems Questionnaire (D. C. Drummond [see PA, Vol 77:22897]). At intake, males had higher expectations than females of help from the AC, while Ss in manual occupations and those in the "less heavy" drinking category had higher EOH from GPs than other patients. By the end of the 1st consultation Ss had raised their rating of problem severity and their EOH from all 3 sources. Overall, patient perceptions of the clinic assessment session were positive. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
