TY  - JOUR
T1  - The use of services for alcohol problems: General practitioner and specialist alcohol clinic
AN  - 618165682; 1992-40673-001
AB  - Examines the influence on service uptake of a number of factors associated with continued attendance at a specialist alcohol clinic, including patient attributes, service activity, patients' self-assessments of their drinking problem, expectations of help (EOH), and perceptions of the services received. 40 patients were assigned to 1 of 2 groups: (1) a clinic group, who received continuing care, and (2) a general practitioner [GP] group, who were asked to return to their GPs. Most Ss attended the recommended source of care at least once following assessment. Ss not returning showed a decrease in their confidence ratings, while those returning showed an increase. Most Ss in Group 1 were positive about the clinic experience. In Group 2, feelings of rapport with the GP and EOH were associated with frequency of attendance. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2019 APA, all rights reserved)
