TY  - JOUR
T1  - General practitioners' experiences of patients with drug and alcohol problems
AN  - 617942942; 1991-22572-001
AB  - Used convergent focus groups to determine what general practitioners (GPs) considered to be the main obstacles and disincentives to the effective management of persons with drug and alcohol problems. Seven groups were conducted, involving 44 GPs whose practices were based in representative parts of metropolitan and rural areas. GPs were more sympathetic to patients with alcohol problems than to those with drug problems, but only some agreed that it was a legitimate area for medical involvement. A provisional typology of GPs was developed with 3 distinct groupings (interactive problem-solvers, traditionalist healers, and technologists), according to the model of medical practice assumed. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
