TY  - JOUR
T1  - Doctors' willingness to intervene in patients' drug and alcohol problems
AN  - 618103833; 1992-14496-001
AB  - Surveyed 103 general practitioners (GPs) to assess the extent of agreement or disagreement with 15 statements originating from an earlier, focus group study (A. M. Roche et al; see record 1991-22572-001) concerning patients' drug and alcohol problems. A cluster analysis indicated the presence of 3 groups of GPs: interactive problem solvers, traditionalist healers, and distant technologists. However, a subsequent principal components analysis and reexamination of the distributions of scores indicated that the survey was measuring a continuous dimension: willingness to intervene. GPs' responses suggested high levels of willingness to intervene, although older GPs showed less willingness to intervene than younger GPs. Implications of these findings for medical educators are discussed in relation to evidence that, in practice, doctors often fail to intervene in patients' drug and alcohol problems. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
