TY  - JOUR
T1  - General practitioner interventions for smoking cessation: Past results and future prospects
AN  - 617936440; 1991-19575-001
AB  - General medical practice is a critical setting for the identification, assessment, treatment, follow-up, and onward referral of smokers. Studies conducted in general practice have shown that general practioners' (GPs) intervention with smokers has a variable degree of success depending on the intensity of contact. Barriers (resistance) by physicians to intervention include lack of belief in own impact, lack of skills training, and concern about offending patients. The stages-of-change framework developed by J. O. Prochaska and C. O. DiClemente (1986), a model used to describe the stages a smoker goes through in the process of becoming a nonsmoker, is applied to GPs' recognition, uptake, and maintenance of smoking cessation interventions. (PsycINFO Database Record (c) 2016 APA, all rights reserved)
