TY  - JOUR
T1  - Attitudes of New Zealand general practitioners to alcohol-related problems
AN  - 616909034; 1984-21469-001
AB  - 431 general practitioners (GPs) responded to a postal questionnaire consisting of attitudinal statements, questions to ascertain knowledge of health risks associated with alcohol use, and questions about each GP's behavior with respect to patients with alcohol problems. The attitudes of this self-selected group indicated majority support for a traditional disease conception of alcoholism embodying abstinence as the goal of treatment. Separate attitudinal factors that emerged represented a modified disease conception, a moralistic attitude, and a belief in a drinking-problem continuum. A majority agreed that abstinence was essential for treatment. The GPs' attitude structure was significantly related to their practice and belief in the efficacy of giving advice to patients with alcohol-related problems. (10 ref) (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2022 APA, all rights reserved)
